- op: create  # de75
  at: '2026-07-03T18:32:08Z'  # de75
  by: agent  # de75
  actor: jgstern_agent  # de75
  clock: 1  # de75
  nonce: de75  # de75
  data:  # de75
    kind: invariant  # de75
    title: "MRO walkers (inherited_calls) can emit a confidently-wrong resolved edge when an EXTERNAL base shadows the in-tree ancestor in the real MRO (external-base blind spot)"  # de75
    status: violated  # de75
    priority: 2  # de75
    parent:  # de75
    tags:  # de75
    - analysis_quality  # de75
    - dispatch  # de75
    isbefore: []  # de75
    duplicate_of: []  # de75
    not_duplicate_of: []  # de75
    pr_ref:  # de75
    description: "Surfaced by the WI-hiziz (D1) C3-walker adversarial review (workflow wf_631a95db-7a2, Verdict 4). PRE-EXISTING in kind: SHARED with every intra-repo-only walker (ruby _walk_insertion_order, java _walk_single_then_interfaces); WI-hiziz newly applies it to Python's _walk_c3. The review classified it a documented NOTE (not a D1 blocker) because it is inherent to intra-repo scope, but it is a WRONG POSITIVE, not mere recall loss.\n\nREPRO (verified end-to-end, /tmp/adj_extbase): `class Base: def pop(self): ...` (in-tree) + `class Sub(dict, Base): pass` + `def use_it(s: Sub): return s.pop()`. Real CPython MRO = [Sub, dict, Base, object]; Sub.pop is dict.pop. hypergumbo emits `use_it -> Base.pop` is_resolved=True conf=0.70 ev=ast_call_inherited_method — WRONG (real dispatch is external dict.pop). Direction matters: `class Sub(Base, dict)` (in-tree base first) -> Base.pop IS correct.\n\nFIX (fast-follow, cheap): gate Site-2 Step-2 (and Site-1/Site-3 once py.py emits their hints) to bias to unresolved when the receiver type's ancestry contains an EXTERNAL/unresolved base positioned ahead of the matched in-tree ancestor — i.e. resolve only when the relevant MRO prefix is fully in-tree. py.py already records a class's declared bases in meta['base_classes']; a base with no corresponding in-tree extends edge is external, so the linker can detect \"this class has an external base that could shadow\" from the child symbol's meta vs its extends edges. Needs measurement (recall cost: many classes extend stdlib/framework bases) before choosing how aggressive the gate is. Applies to the C3 walker AND the pre-existing ruby/java walkers."  # de75
    fields:  # de75
      statement: "The inherited_calls MRO walkers resolve a receiver's inherited method over the IN-TREE ancestor subgraph only. An EXTERNAL base (builtin/stdlib/3rd-party — dict, Enum, Exception, a framework base) that sits AHEAD of the in-tree ancestor in the real MRO and defines the same method name is invisible to the walk, so the walker resolves to the wrong (in-tree) method — a confidently-wrong resolved calls edge (false reachability of the in-tree method that HIDES the real external dispatch). Resolution MUST bias to unresolved when an external base could shadow the matched in-tree ancestor."  # de75
      root_cause: "External bases produce no extends edge (py.py _resolve_base_class resolves only in-tree bases), so the inheritance_index the walkers traverse omits them; the C3 / insertion-order / single-then-interfaces walkers linearize the in-tree subgraph only and cannot know an external base precedes the in-tree ancestor in the true MRO."  # de75
- op: discuss  # 10b1
  at: '2026-07-03T22:02:16Z'  # 10b1
  by: agent  # 10b1
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 10b1
  clock: 2  # 10b1
  nonce: 10b1  # 10b1
  message: "ESCALATION (WI-hiziz PR-2, PR #409): Site-1 (self.method()) is now LIVE for Python, so this external-base blind spot's fix scope now genuinely includes Site-1 — and PR-2 aims Site-1 at the MOST COMMON trigger shape (a class that subclasses a framework base like Model/BaseModel/Enum AND an in-tree mixin, calling self.method()), materially increasing reachability of the wrong-positive. The PR-2 adversarial review (wf_e6f81f04-d63) independently re-confirmed FM1 and it ships documented-eyes-open via test_site1_python_external_base_mixin_known_gap (asserts the current possibly-wrong in-tree bind, ref this item). The fix (bias to unresolved when an external base could shadow the matched in-tree ancestor) should now cover Site-1 + Site-2 Step-2 uniformly across the C3/ruby/java walkers. Recommend raising priority given the increased reachability."  # 10b1
- op: discuss  # d34c
  at: '2026-07-05T07:11:27Z'  # d34c
  by: agent  # d34c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d34c
  clock: 3  # d34c
  nonce: d34c  # d34c
  message: "Design + adversarial-review workflow (5 agents; full analysis saved to ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/design_analyses/inv-guviv-external-base-gate-design_07052026.json) shows a naive positional gate is NOT a clean fix. Two independent designs (per-class direct-base-order path check; spine-scoped MRO-prefix check) were BOTH rated 'both' (residual-wrong-edge AND over-suppresses). Decisive findings: (1) OVER-SUPPRESSION — external method tables are invisible, so 'external base positionally ahead of the resolver' is NECESSARY but nowhere near SUFFICIENT for an actual shadow; a hard bias-to-unresolved SYSTEMATICALLY kills the DOMINANT external-mixin-first idiom (Django/DRF 'class UserViewSet(LoginRequiredMixin, AuditMixin)' calling self.log_action() provably binds the in-tree AuditMixin.log_action, but the gate suppresses because the auth mixin sits at base index 0). The codebase's own inherited_calls test 'test_site1_python_external_base_mixin_known_gap' uses exactly this external-first shape as canonical FM1. (2) RESIDUAL WRONG EDGE — the local per-class positional test misses C3 sibling-subtree interleaving: a plain 2-parent diamond 'class D(Left, R)' with 'Left(ExtMixin)' where ExtMixin defines the method has true MRO [D,Left,ExtMixin,R] binding ExtMixin.m, but the gate keeps the wrong D->R.m edge. PRINCIPLED FIX PATHS (deferred, more involved than a positional gate): (a) catalog-gated suppression — only suppress when the ahead-external is a KNOWN base (dict/OrderedDict/list/Enum/known ORM/serializer bases via _third_party_bases.py) that actually defines the called method NAME, so auth/permission mixins never trigger; and/or (b) confidence-DOWNGRADE instead of hard suppression (emit the edge at lower confidence + 'possible-external-shadow' annotation), preserving recall. Staying violated; a quick positional gate would REGRESS recall and must not ship."  # d34c
- op: discuss  # 6aa3
  at: '2026-07-06T04:13:25Z'  # 6aa3
  by: agent  # 6aa3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6aa3
  clock: 4  # 6aa3
  nonce: 6aa3  # 6aa3
  message: "Scoped fix landed (PR merged, dev e278e0b85d). Added a hardcoded _STDLIB_BASE_METHODS catalog (builtin base short name to its named methods; sibling of DJANGO_BASE_METHODS / THIRD_PARTY_BASE_METHODS, hardcoded over YAML per ADR-0029 static-language-semantics rule) driving _python_stdlib_base_shadows in inherited_calls.py. When a cataloged builtin (dict/list/set/frozenset/tuple/str/bytes/bytearray) that DEFINES the called method is declared BEFORE the first in-tree base of the class, the Python inherited resolution is biased to unresolved - the real target is a builtin method the in-tree-only C3 walk cannot see. Applied at Site-1 (self.method()) and Site-2 Step-2 (typed receiver), Python-only. SOUND BY CONSTRUCTION: never over-suppresses the dominant external-mixin-first idiom (a non-stdlib mixin like LoginRequiredMixin is deliberately NOT cataloged - the exact over-suppression the naive any-external-ahead gate would have caused, which the design workflow rejected); it fires only when a cataloged builtin that defines the method precedes any in-tree base; a class defining the method itself, a builtin after the in-tree base, and a builtin not defining the method are exempt. An alignment guard (every in-tree parent name must appear among the declared bases) biases to no-shadow under import-alias name divergence, closing an adversarial-review over-suppression finding. 11 TDD tests (the bug + every no-over-suppression guard + the alias case + the residual + name-mismatch); adversarial review (1 agent, 6 attack vectors) found NO realistic over-suppression; 100 percent core-package coverage. STAYS violated on documented residuals: (1) a generic 3rd-party/ORM external base (e.g. ext.SqlBase) is NOT cataloged - the catalog is deliberately not open-ended to avoid over-suppression - a fast-follow could extend it to well-known ORM bases if a bakeoff shows real hits (the existing test_site1_python_external_base_mixin_known_gap documents this residual, unchanged); (2) Site-3 (self.field.method() inherited field) not yet gated (mechanical - same helper, one call site); (3) multi-level ancestor shadowing and the [Mixin, builtin, Mixin2] interleave are UNDER-suppression residuals (safe - leave the pre-existing edge, never over-suppress). Full-close path = this scoped stdlib catalog + optional ORM-base extension + Site-3 coverage."  # 6aa3
